```text
Refactor Touch Mapper’s browser-side map-description code so that feature grouping and semantic deduplication never depend on translated or formatted display text.

First read AGENTS.md and the relevant documentation under doc/, particularly:
- development-conventions.md
- map-description-model-schema.md
- map-content-verification.md
- map-description-introspection.md
- converter-pipeline-stages.md

Implement and verify the refactor. Do not merely produce a plan.

## Context

Touch Mapper produces tactile maps for blind and visually impaired users. It also presents a textual description containing roads, paths, railways, waterways, water areas, buildings, and points of interest.

The converter supplies structured feature data in map-content.json. The browser selects, groups, combines, and translates that information into readable descriptions.

This architecture should remain intact. The problem is that some browser code translates information too early and subsequently uses the resulting text as a grouping or deduplication key.

Examples include:
- Combining a building group’s member locations and edge contacts by comparing their formatted phrases.
- Grouping unnamed water areas by a formatted location description.
- Grouping unnamed waterways by formatted route/location text.
- Grouping unnamed POIs by formatted location text or localized type labels.
- Merging unnamed linear features using formatted route descriptions, edge descriptions, and lengths.

Consequently, wording changes or language differences can affect which features are combined or which facts survive.

Some code already uses structured identities. For example, road-route deduplication compares unordered endpoint-location pairs. Preserve that behavior and reuse the underlying approach where appropriate.

## Objective

Make semantic decisions independent of presentation language:

1. Select the meaning to describe from the existing structured input.
2. Combine and deduplicate language-independent descriptions.
3. Translate and format only after those decisions are complete.

Changing the locale, wording, capitalization, punctuation, grammatical form, or number formatting must not change:
- Feature group membership.
- Counts.
- Coverage or length totals.
- Filtering identities.
- Which location, route, edge-contact, or connection facts survive.

Natural changes in output wording and ordering caused solely by localized presentation remain permissible where already intended. Do not make semantic deduplication depend on that ordering.

## Scope and non-goals

Inspect the entire browser map-description path, not just the recently added building-group code.

Likely starting points:
- web/src/scripts/map-desc-ways.js
- web/src/scripts/map-desc-areas.js
- web/src/scripts/map-desc-pois.js
- web/src/scripts/map-description.js

Search for translated text used as map keys, set membership, equality tests, normalized signatures, and merge criteria. Function names may include locationKey, normalizeMergeText, unnamedMergeSignature, or similar; inspect actual behavior rather than relying on names.

This is a semantic/presentation separation refactor, not a redesign of descriptions.

Do not:
- Change converter pipeline responsibilities.
- Change tactile geometry, feature classification, content modes, or map filtering.
- Introduce React or another frontend framework.
- Reconstruct route connectivity or add geometry-union algorithms.
- Change which buildings are grouped by address.
- Add account or map-history features.
- Rewrite Git history.
- Merge genuinely distinct feature types just because their translated labels are identical.
- Infer unsupported facts when source data is missing.

Existing map-content.json should contain the necessary structured information. Prefer a browser-only solution without changing its schema or regenerating maps. If you discover a genuine missing-data blocker, explain it before expanding the scope.

## Existing behaviors that must be preserved

### Linear features

Road/route descriptions can contain multiple visible segments.

Current intended behavior:
- Describe every distinct visible segment location pair.
- Treat A→B and B→A as the same approximate location pair.
- Retain the first encountered direction for wording.
- Suppress a standalone location if that location is already covered by a retained pair.
- Preserve unrelated standalone locations.
- Include edge crossings from all visible segments.
- Do not infer a continuous route between disconnected segments.
- Preserve existing intersection/connection reporting.
- Railway junction narration is intentionally suppressed and must remain suppressed.
- Unnamed road/path aggregate summaries are a separate product decision; do not change their content as part of this refactor.

### Existing multi-building groups

Current intended behavior for groups with more than one building:
- Explicit localized count and plural type in the heading.
- Every distinct member location description.
- Every distinct member edge-contact description.
- Sum all members’ coverage percentages and format the total once.
- If any member’s coverage is missing or invalid, omit the total rather than presenting a partial sum as complete.
- Do not show single-building shape, orientation, or component-count details for the group.
- Preserve all member filter references.
- Preserve existing group membership.

Singleton building descriptions must remain unchanged.

The UI currently sums the coverage values provided by the converter, which may already be rounded. Do not invent additional precision or change this into geometric union coverage.

For edge contacts, preserve distinct member facts. Do not blindly sum edge-contact percentages because spans may overlap.

### Other areas and POIs

Preserve the current intended grouping categories, counts, filtering references, and aggregation behavior while replacing text-based equivalence with structured equivalence.

Do not opportunistically fix unrelated area-aggregation issues during this refactor. Document any you discover.

## Implementation approach

Use small, explicit, language-independent description structures.

Conceptually, these may represent:
- A single approximate location.
- A route between two approximate locations.
- A dominant region with a secondary extension.
- Two similarly occupied regions.
- Several regions or a distributed area.
- An edge crossing/contact, with its edge, position qualifiers, and applicable measurement.
- A connection to an identified feature or feature type.

These are examples, not a requirement to create a large generic framework. Inspect the existing selectors and represent the actual cases they support.

### Separate selection from formatting

The area coverage formatter is particularly important. It currently selects among several kinds of location sentences while constructing translated text.

Split that responsibility:
- One function selects a structured description from coverage/location data.
- Another formats the selected description using the existing translations.

Preserve the existing thresholds, priorities, fallback behavior, and region-selection rules unless a change is strictly necessary to remove language dependence.

Apply the same separation to relevant way and POI code.

### Define equivalence explicitly

Do not deduplicate raw geometry objects or complete raw records. Compare the semantic information selected for presentation.

Examples:
- Slightly different area coverage distributions may legitimately select the same “mostly in A, extending toward B” description.
- Route endpoint pairs are unordered for deduplication, but the first encountered orientation can be retained for wording.
- Dominant and secondary regions are not interchangeable: “mostly A, extending toward B” differs from “mostly B, extending toward A.”
- A corner, a nearby edge, and an interior part must remain distinct where the current model distinguishes them.
- Different edge-contact measurements must not disappear merely because formatting rounds them to the same displayed value, unless the intended equivalence explicitly uses a documented numeric display bucket.
- Missing or invalid structured information must not cause unrelated features to collapse into one generic group.

Use canonical, deterministic keys built from explicit fields. Do not rely on incidental object property order.

If current merging intentionally depends on displayed length or another rounded measurement, make the numeric bucketing rule explicit and locale-independent. Preserve the existing precision rule where reasonable; do not use the formatted string as its identity.

### Avoid textual fallback identities

Do not replace text-based deduplication with structured keys that quietly fall back to translated phrases.

When information is insufficient for safe semantic comparison:
- Keep features/descriptions separate, or
- Use a stable feature identity where that is appropriate.

Document the choice and test it.

Distinguish source names from presentation text. Comparing actual source names or stable feature IDs may be legitimate; comparing translated type labels or rendered sentences is not.

### Keep formatting at the boundary

Translation functions should not be needed to decide semantic group membership or equivalence.

Preserve useful styling and accessibility structure in the existing render-ready models. The final text should still flow through the established translation/template mechanism; there is no complete runtime locale dictionary.

Do not add a second independent set of location-selection rules solely to compute keys. The same structured description should drive both equivalence and formatting.

## Regression tests

Add small offline behavioral tests to the quick regression suite and register new checks in test/regression/run.py.

Use actual production description code, not a reimplementation of the algorithm in tests.

Existing relevant tests include:
- test/regression/way_segments.js and its fragmented-road fixture.
- test/regression/way_plurals.js.
- test/regression/area_groups.js.
- test/regression/area_groups.json.
- test/regression/area_groups_baseline.json.
- Existing naming, content-filtering, converter, and browser checks.

Inspect the current repository because filenames or coverage may have evolved.

Required coverage:

1. Locale independence
   Run the same structured features through every supported locale:
   en, fi, de, es, nl.
   Assert identical semantic grouping, counts, totals, member/filter identities,
   and retained location facts.

2. Deliberate translation collisions
   Supply translations that make semantically different regions or feature
   types render identically.
   Assert that distinct semantic descriptions/features are not merged.

3. Different wording for equivalent meanings
   Change grammatical forms, punctuation, capitalization, or equivalent
   from/to wording.
   Assert that semantic duplicates still deduplicate.

4. Fragmented roads
   Preserve the existing complex multi-segment regression:
   reversed endpoint pairs and repeated covered locations reduce to the
   expected distinct pairs, with no lost unrelated location facts.
   Include repeated and differently ordered input segments.

5. Grouped buildings
   Preserve the anonymized multi-building fixture:
   explicit counts, all unique member locations/contacts, summed coverage,
   no group shape/orientation, unchanged filter references.
   Preserve singleton and unrelated water-area baselines.

6. Unnamed linear features and waterways
   Exercise former text-based route/edge/length merge paths.
   Include equivalent facts in different input orders, distinct edges,
   different feature types, and measurements around rounding boundaries.

7. Water areas
   Cover single-region, dominant/secondary, comparable-region, multi-region,
   and distributed descriptions.
   Prove that equivalent selected descriptions can merge despite irrelevant
   raw-data differences, while different selected meanings remain distinct.

8. POIs
   Cover unnamed POI grouping by structured location and type.
   Ensure identical translated labels do not merge distinct types.

9. Missing and malformed data
   No crashes, unresolved placeholders, invented locations, or accidental
   grouping of unrelated features with unknown locations.

10. Display compatibility
    Keep existing expected wording where semantics are unchanged.
    Review any differences explicitly; do not blindly regenerate snapshots
    to make tests pass.

Prefer assertions on structured meaning plus selected exact-text checks. Avoid making the new tests depend entirely on translated prose.

## Privacy requirements

All permanent regression fixtures and documentation must use synthetic addresses, names, map IDs, feature IDs, and links.

Do not include:
- A real user-provided address.
- A source map URL or ID.
- Original OSM IDs from a user-provided map.
- Comments explaining which real location a fixture came from.
- Request metadata, IP addresses, fingerprints, or other identifying metadata.

Use the existing anonymized fixtures. Do not recover their original identities from temporary files or Git history.

If new examples are needed, construct synthetic structured inputs rather than fetching a real user map.

Do not copy ignored temporary artifacts into the repository without inspecting and sanitizing them.

## Verification

Follow the repository’s operational instructions.

At minimum:
- Run make test-regression; keep it offline and below the repository’s time limit.
- Run make test-regression-full.
- Build the local UI.
- Inspect relevant description output in all five languages.
- Capture and visually inspect desktop and narrow/mobile screenshots of
  representative road, grouped-building, water-area, and POI descriptions.
- Check wrapping, clipping, heading/list structure, and any affected keyboard
  interactions.
- Keep the local preview running afterward.

Use the required sandbox/network permissions for local browser tests as
documented in AGENTS.md. Do not deploy unless explicitly asked.

## Documentation and handoff

Document:
- The new separation between semantic description selection and formatting.
- Canonical equivalence rules, especially unordered route pairs versus
  ordered dominant/secondary regions.
- Measurement bucketing, if needed.
- Behavior when structured data is missing.

In the completion report:
- Summarize the text-based grouping/deduplication paths that were removed.
- State any remaining uses of text comparison and explain why they are
  identity/name comparisons rather than semantic-description comparisons.
- Report tests and visual checks.
- This is a behavior-preserving refactor. Do not change map-content.json, converter behavior, or user-visible descriptions and interactions. Treat existing output as the compatibility baseline. If semantic deduplication exposes a conflict with existing behavior, report it and obtain a decision before changing that behavior. Do not update expected output or visual snapshots merely to accommodate the refactor.
- Confirm that no converter/schema changes were needed, or explain any
  previously approved exception.
- Confirm that permanent fixtures contain no source-map identifying data.

Preserve unrelated worktree changes and do not commit or deploy automatically.
